-- title:
--   $F$ consists exactly of the piecewise-linear maps
-- statement:
--   An order isomorphism of $[0,1]$ belongs to Thompson's group $F$ if and only if it is
--   piecewise linear with finitely many breakpoints, all breakpoints dyadic and every slope a power
--   of two.
--
--   $F$ is defined as the subgroup *generated by* the maps with that property, so one direction is
--   immediate. The content is the other: that the maps with that property are already closed under
--   composition and inverses, so that generating adds nothing. This is what Cannon-Floyd-Parry
--   establish at the end of their section 1, by way of the fact that such a map carries the dyadic
--   rationals bijectively onto themselves.
-- source:
--   Cannon, J. W., Floyd, W. J., Parry, W. R., Introductory notes on Richard Thompson's groups, L'Enseignement Mathematique (2) 42 (1996) 215-256, https://doi.org/10.5169/seals-87877, section 1, p. 217, 'Thus F is a subgroup of the group of all homeomorphisms from [0,1] to [0,1]'

import Definitions.Def_CannonFloydParry
import Mathlib

namespace CannonFloydParry

theorem mem_F_iff_isThompson {f : UI ≃o UI} : f ∈ F ↔ IsThompson f := by
  sorry

end CannonFloydParry
